{"prompt": "A shirt costs $25 after a 20% discount was applied twice. What was the original price?", "response": "Let me work through this carefully. Two successive 20% discounts means the price was multiplied by 0.8 twice: original x 0.8 x 0.8 = original x 0.64. So original = 25 / 0.64 = 39.0625. Check: 39.0625 x 0.8 = 31.25, and 31.25 x 0.8 = 25. Correct. The original price was $39.06 (to the cent)."}
{"prompt": "If all Bloops are Razzies and no Razzies are Lazzies, can a Bloop be a Lazzie?", "response": "Step 1: every Bloop is a Razzie. Step 2: no Razzie is a Lazzie, so the sets Razzie and Lazzie are disjoint. Step 3: since Bloops sit entirely inside Razzies, and Razzies never overlap Lazzies, Bloops cannot overlap Lazzies either. So no — a Bloop cannot be a Lazzie."}
{"prompt": "A train leaves station A heading east at 60 mph. Two hours later, another train leaves station A heading east at 90 mph. How many hours after the second train departs will it catch up to the first train?", "response": "Let me solve this step by step. Train 1 has a 2-hour head start at 60 mph, so when Train 2 departs, Train 1 is already 60 × 2 = 120 miles ahead. Train 2 travels at 90 mph while Train 1 continues at 60 mph, so Train 2 closes the gap at 90 - 60 = 30 mph. To close a 120-mile gap at 30 mph, it takes 120 / 30 = 4 hours. Let me verify: after 4 hours, Train 2 has gone 90 × 4 = 360 miles, and Train 1 has gone 60 × (2 + 4) = 60 × 6 = 360 miles. They meet. The answer is 4 hours."}
{"prompt": "You have a 3-gallon jug and a 5-gallon jug. How can you measure exactly 4 gallons of water?", "response": "Let me think through this methodically. I need to get exactly 4 gallons using only a 3-gallon and 5-gallon jug. Here is a step-by-step procedure: Step 1: Fill the 5-gallon jug completely (5 gal in jug-5, 0 gal in jug-3). Step 2: Pour from jug-5 into jug-3 until jug-3 is full. This leaves 2 gallons in jug-5 (2 gal in jug-5, 3 gal in jug-3). Step 3: Empty jug-3 (2 gal in jug-5, 0 gal in jug-3). Step 4: Pour the 2 gallons from jug-5 into jug-3 (0 gal in jug-5, 2 gal in jug-3). Step 5: Fill jug-5 completely again (5 gal in jug-5, 2 gal in jug-3). Step 6: Pour from jug-5 into jug-3 until jug-3 is full. Jug-3 had 2 gallons, so it needs exactly 1 more gallon to be full. Pouring 1 gallon from jug-5 leaves exactly 4 gallons in jug-5. The answer: 4 gallons remain in the 5-gallon jug."}
{"prompt": "A factory produces widgets. Each day it produces 20% more than the day before. On Monday it produced 100 widgets. How many widgets were produced from Monday through Friday?", "response": "Let me calculate day by day. Monday: 100 widgets (given). Tuesday: 100 × 1.20 = 120 widgets. Wednesday: 120 × 1.20 = 144 widgets. Thursday: 144 × 1.20 = 172.8, which rounds to 173 widgets (you can only produce whole widgets). Friday: 173 × 1.20 = 207.6, which rounds to 208 widgets. Now summing: 100 + 120 + 144 + 173 + 208 = 745. Let me double-check the addition: 100 + 120 = 220, 220 + 144 = 364, 364 + 173 = 537, 537 + 208 = 745. The factory produced 745 widgets from Monday through Friday."}
{"prompt": "In a group of 30 people, 18 speak English, 15 speak French, and 7 speak neither language. How many people speak both English and French?", "response": "Let me work through this using set theory. We know that 7 people speak neither English nor French, so the number of people who speak at least one language is 30 - 7 = 23. Using the inclusion-exclusion principle: |E ∪ F| = |E| + |F| - |E ∩ F|, where E is the set of English speakers and F is the set of French speakers. We know |E ∪ F| = 23, |E| = 18, and |F| = 15. So: 23 = 18 + 15 - |E ∩ F|. This gives: 23 = 33 - |E ∩ F|, so |E ∩ F| = 33 - 23 = 10. Let me verify: 10 speak both, 8 speak only English (18 - 10), 5 speak only French (15 - 10), and 7 speak neither. Total: 10 + 8 + 5 + 7 = 30. The answer is 10 people speak both English and French."}
{"prompt": "A right triangle has a perimeter of 24 cm and a hypotenuse of 10 cm. What are the lengths of the other two sides?", "response": "Let me solve this systematically. Let the two legs be a and b, and the hypotenuse is c = 10. From the perimeter: a + b + c = 24, so a + b = 24 - 10 = 14. From the Pythagorean theorem: a² + b² = c² = 100. I have two equations: (1) a + b = 14, and (2) a² + b² = 100. From equation (1), b = 14 - a. Substituting into equation (2): a² + (14 - a)² = 100. Expanding: a² + 196 - 28a + a² = 100. Simplifying: 2a² - 28a + 196 = 100, so 2a² - 28a + 96 = 0. Dividing by 2: a² - 14a + 48 = 0. Using the quadratic formula or factoring: (a - 6)(a - 8) = 0, so a = 6 or a = 8. If a = 6, then b = 14 - 6 = 8. If a = 8, then b = 14 - 8 = 6. Either way, the two legs are 6 cm and 8 cm. Let me verify: 6² + 8² = 36 + 64 = 100 = 10² ✓, and 6 + 8 + 10 = 24 ✓. The other two sides are 6 cm and 8 cm."}
